# Property Managers: Automate the Rent Cycle (and Stop Chasing Payments)

Property management is one of the most automatable businesses in Nigeria, and almost nobody has automated it.

The reason is structural, and it's good news: **the work is a loop, and the loop repeats every month, forever.**

Enquiries come in. Inspections get scheduled. Rent is due. Payments arrive unevenly. Someone reconciles what came in against what should have. Listings get refreshed so vacancies fill.

Same sequence. Every month. Indefinitely.

That's not a burden — that's the clearest automation case there is, because you can price the cycle and count the cycles.

## The Five Stages, And Where Each One Leaks

### 1. Enquiry Triage (WhatsApp and Calls)

Enquiries arrive on WhatsApp, phone, and sometimes email. Someone reads each one, decides what it's about, and routes it.

**The leak:** this is the highest-volume, lowest-judgement task in the business. Most enquiries are the same three questions. Yet each one consumes a person's attention to answer something already answered forty times.

**Automated:** an agent reads the incoming message, classifies it (viewing request / price question / maintenance / existing tenant), answers the routine ones instantly from your actual listing data, and routes only the genuinely non-standard ones to a human.

### 2. Inspection Scheduling

Viewing requests get arranged by message. Back and forth to agree a time, then someone updates a calendar, then confirms again.

**The leak:** the coordination cost per viewing often exceeds the viewing itself. And double-bookings on the same unit cost you a second trip from an already-interested prospect.

**Automated:** self-service booking against real availability. The prospect picks a slot, the system confirms, reminders go out automatically, no-shows become visible instead of anecdotal.

### 3. Rent Reconciliation

Payments arrive by transfer, at different times, sometimes split, sometimes late, often without a reference that matches anything.

**The leak:** this is the expensive one. Reconciliation is manual matching — payments against units against tenants — and it's done *every month*. A team of two can spend several days a month on it, and the errors it produces (a payment credited to the wrong unit) take longer to unwind than the reconciliation took to do.

**Automated:** payments match against expected rent per unit automatically. Matches confirm silently. **Exceptions get flagged** — the short payment, the missing one, the over-payment — so a human only touches what actually needs judgement.

This is the single highest-value automation in property management, and it's not close.

### 4. Listing Syndication

A unit goes live. It has to appear on every portal you use, and stay consistent across them.

**The leak:** posted once, then re-posted manually, then updated manually when the price changes, then taken down manually when it lets. Each cycle is repeated effort on information that only exists in one place.

**Automated:** post once, syndicate everywhere. Price change propagates. Let status propagates.

### 5. Guest And Viewing Access

Short-lets and managed buildings have people arriving who need access, verified, without someone standing there to do it.

**Automated:** QR-based access — the same engine behind our event check-in work. 50,000+ verified records, 0.4-second resolution, and a full audit trail of who entered and when.

## Why This Vertical Pays Back Fastest

Because of the loop.

A one-off automation has to justify itself once against an uncertain future. A **monthly** one has a cycle count you can divide straight into the build cost.

Take rent reconciliation alone. Three staff-days a month at a Nigerian loaded rate is a real annual figure — and it recurs whether or not you act. Automate it against a build that's a fraction of that, and the payback lands inside a quarter.

That's the whole argument. Not "AI is the future." Just: *here is a cost that repeats every month, here is what closing it costs, here is when it pays for itself.*

## Where To Start

Do not start with syndication, however tempting the visible win is.

Start with **rent reconciliation**, because it's the highest-frequency, highest-error, highest-cost stage, and because its exceptions are well-defined — which makes it the easiest thing to automate correctly.

Then enquiry triage, because it's the highest-volume.

Then inspections. Then access.

Sequencing matters more than scope. One stage automated properly beats four automated badly, and the first one funds the rest.

